And if a person acts like that, without a shadow, without a doubt, they're very hard to be around because they never see themselves at fault. They never doubt themselves.

00:34:06.387 --> 00:34:23.875
And Jung says it will not only be tough for them, let's say, to find friends and interact with others, but also the psyche will constantly push against that and say like, you're not as great as you think you are. Of course, this can go to the completely other side when you completely live in the shadow, like Ross.

00:34:24.372 --> 00:34:47.176
Being completely unsure and very doubtful and not even aware of your good qualities. It has to be both. You have to be aware of the good and the bad qualities at the same time, making it very, very hard. And this, with a man becomes for himself the most difficult problem, also ties very nicely with Nietzsche. Jung was a huge fan of Nietzsche.

00:34:47.241 --> 00:35:09.298
He had an incredibly long seminar he gave on Thus Spoke Zarathustra. And there's a famous quote by Nietzsche about, you try to find the heaviest burden and you found yourself, which is an interesting way to look at it. And this is psychology. So if a person is more or less complete, his shadow side is visible.

00:35:09.605 --> 00:35:32.134
If it is not, you feel that person is painted flat upon the wall with more or less shadow. There are more or less negotiation or contradiction. And without that, nobody is complete. People who have only two dimensions are identical with a sort of persona or mask, which they carry in front of themselves and behind which they hide. The persona in itself casts no shadow.

00:35:33.810 --> 00:35:47.956
Persona, meaning the role that we have in the world, the way how we are perceived. For example, when you're a policeman and you're only a policeman, or you're a a mother and you're only a mother. This is then the persona. This is what people can see from, from outside.

00:35:50.101 --> 00:36:09.773
And it's, let's say, very easy to hide the complexity of one's own psyche behind that, behind a function, let's say. And people notice. That's the thing. You don't notice your shadow, but everybody else does. This is also why Jung says the easiest way to find out about your shadow is just to ask other people what's wrong about yourself or what you don't know about yourself.

00:36:11.598 --> 00:36:29.997
Which is an interesting experience, to say the least. But what we're talking about here, integration, development, Jung has a term for that, and that term is called individuation, which is the individual journey one takes through one's psyche, through one's life, and expressions one's psyche through time.

00:36:32.280 --> 00:36:55.217
And the integration of the shadow, of course, is called shadow integration. And for the idea to say that the shadow is associated with doubt and having too much doubt or having no doubt is a bad position to be in. It's important to take doubt into consideration.

00:36:56.088 --> 00:37:11.658
And we have the smartest person who lived at Greece at the time, Socrates, being told that by the Oracle of Delphi, has this very famous quote, I know that I know nothing. This is already a doubtful position.

00:37:11.771 --> 00:37:34.908
It's not a bad idea to start from that point for one's own psychological development, to be humble and to be doubtful about oneself. Not too doubtful, not to be Ross, but to be questioning oneself. So how would it look like, this integration of the shadow? Because I talked before, it's a, it's a figure.

00:37:35.320 --> 00:37:54.615
It's a representation of psychological contents that you encounter in your life, in your dreams, in your psyche. And Jung says the integration is interesting in the sense of that it's a relationship. You're kind of building a relationship with yourself, with different parts within you.

00:37:56.625 --> 00:38:15.926
This is why he says in this quote, there is no general effective technique for assimilating the shadow. It is more like diplomacy or statesmanship, and it is always an individual matter. First, one has to accept and take seriously the existence of the shadow, the existence of the other part one is not aware of.

00:38:16.295 --> 00:38:37.907
Second, one has to become aware of its qualities and intentions, meaning Is it good quality? Is it bad quality? That happens through conscientious attention to moods, fantasy, and impulses. Again, perceiving ourselves in the psyche, inside, outside, dreams, persons that we have strong emotional reactions to.

00:38:39.403 --> 00:39:00.786
Third, a long process of negotiation is unavoidable. This is kind of a process that has to be lived through again and again. It is, Jungian psychology is very, very dynamic. Things constantly shift, constantly change, and it kind of needs a maintenance that's going on.

00:39:01.637 --> 00:39:19.397
Just like a normal relationship that you have with any other person. When you're friends or partner with someone, that's not it. You have to keep it up. And same way, you have to be Checking in with yourself, let's say, and with the different things that you are maybe not aware of right now.

00:39:22.638 --> 00:39:45.561
So interestingly enough, we have this then also in movies, also with the opposites as said before, but also then with this doubt that you have two characters that learn about themselves through other people that are very different from themselves. And I have here some examples from The Men in Black or from Crazy Stupid Love or from Rush Hour.

00:39:46.027 --> 00:40:07.412
And it is, it's interesting to see how even the facial expressions are very, very similar, but you become aware of the ways how you are incomplete, especially in Crazy Stupid Love. You have this very, very sure of himself Ryan Gosling character, and then you have Steve Carell who kind of has to learn how to deal with women. And both learn from each other, right?

00:40:07.476 --> 00:40:19.474
Both are incomplete in their own way, but they complete each other through a relationship. And this is a very important part about shadow integration or any kind of integration. The parts are projected outwards.

00:40:20.067 --> 00:40:38.856
That means you kind of build a relationship then with this person, but you not only have then a relationship with this person, but you build the relationship with yourself in a way. So shadow integration has to do with another person. But this not only makes good between you and the person, but kind of makes good inside of you.

00:40:40.510 --> 00:40:59.534
It's everything very, ah, physical again with, with, with Jung. And similar then at the next step for anima and animus, this then with the opposite sex. So the first one can be a friend with a shadow. The other one is then rather with a partner, with a long-term stable relationship.

00:41:02.525 --> 00:41:24.468
And this is then also where things would progress towards if we would say, okay, now we became aware of our shadow. We are aware of its qualities. We're constantly negotiating. We're exchanging gifts and compliments. What is happening then psychologically? Because I want to go into that also. What is the stage beyond shadow integration?

00:41:26.459 --> 00:41:38.592
Our consciousness becomes larger. Now we have the shadow and it's kind of in our consciousness, it's integrated, it's part of it. And we are now, let's say, completely aware and have fully mapped our personal unconscious.

00:41:38.656 --> 00:41:58.174
So the things in our conscious that are personally related to us and that were before in the shadow, that where the psyche sent the figure of the shadow to us to make us aware of these things, this is now all there. But the unconscious does not stop. It's just that we now perceive other parts of the unconscious more clearly.

00:41:59.840 --> 00:42:21.594
This part of the unconscious is the collective unconscious. And this is anima and animus. And this is when it becomes super freaky. And this is where it becomes very, very hard to explain what this is actually. What is the anima? What is the animus? And what are their expressions expressing because they're kind of functions.

00:42:21.658 --> 00:42:44.006
And also the collective unconscious, that's a scary part to become aware of all the impersonal things inside your psyche that you're not aware of, or like that you cannot really comprehend. And I think the easiest way and the easiest way to explain it is emotions. Emotions are highly, highly impersonal.

00:42:47.384 --> 00:43:12.191
Fear, anger, lust, joy, all these different things kind of grip a person and drag them in a certain direction. And we, in these situations, are so identified with that that we think, oh, this is us. And actually it's not really. And it's then especially dangerous when we blindly act out anger, for example.

00:43:14.840 --> 00:43:38.266
So a well-integrated person still feels anger, but they do not act on that. They are, let's say, socialized enough not to just blindly act out their anger. It's still inside of them, but it's not being acted out. And to have this awareness that this is actually pretty impersonal and does not, like, anger does not care about you.

00:43:38.952 --> 00:43:57.817
Fear does not care about you. And the other drives also. And this, you would now have to go even deeper into anima, animus, and also collective unconscious where you have the whole history and psychological development of humanity at itself, which would be a bit too much for today.

00:43:58.557 --> 00:44:20.600
But I kind of wanted to hint at that, what lies beyond the shadow if one build that relationship because then you hit the anima, animus problem. And Jung points out why it's so important to move beyond the shadow, to move beyond the point where you think it's all personal in your unconscious.

00:44:24.330 --> 00:44:37.157
